  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 346 PM EDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nToday, visible satellite and RAP analysis show high pressure \nbuilding into the region behind yesterday's trough, now sitting over \nLake Michigan and Michigan's lower peninsula. Temperatures are \nreaching into the high 70s across the UP, except for some coastal \nlocations with light onshore winds and subsequently lower \ntemperatures in the high 60s and low 70s. While today's highs \nare expected to remain very near climatology, anticipate \nslightly above normal highs in the low- to mid-80s for tomorrow \nand Friday. This will be due a shot of warm low-level air \ndetaching itself from Four Corners high and riding \nsouthwesterly flow to Upper Midwest ahead of an embedded \nshortwave. In addition, weak ridging & anticyclonic flow will \nlead to southerly winds over the UP, resulting in downsloping on\nLake Superior shorelines. Thus, expect the highest temperatures\nto be found along the Lake Superior shoreline for the next two \ndays.\n\nAt a larger scale, GOES water vapor imagery shows that the mid-level \nCanadian low has set up in conjunction with a Four Corners high, \nleading to SW'ly geostrophic flow over the region. As weak ridging \ntoday yields to a relatively tight pressure gradient on Thursday \nmorning ahead of an embedded shortwave, land areas may see periods \nof higher-than-normal wind speeds. Friday looks be the day of \nhighest winds, with widespread southerly gusts at 20-25 mph during \nthe afternoon ahead of a cold front. \n\nFriday's embedded shortwave brings with it notable precipitation and \nthunderstorm chances in the afternoon and evening. For moisture, the \nEPS and GEFS have 1-1.5\" of PWAT while the CMCE has over 1.5\". The \nNBM runs from 24 hours previous have upped the 6-hr (18Z-00Z Friday) \nmean QPF totals from 0.02\"-0.13 to 0.02\"-0.40\". The NBM 90th \npercentile distributions remain around 0.50\", and localized maximums \nsit upwards of 1\". High PWATs, the trend in recent model runs, and \nhigh-end totals lend confidence to the possibility of localized \nhigher rainfall during the afternoon and evening hours. For severe \npotential, the ingredients are in place but largely lacking in \nmagnitude. While good model agreement exists that the UP will be \nunder RR entrance region of an upper level jet Friday afternoon, the \nspeed of this jet is lackluster and positioning yields only speed \nshear. NBM mean MUCAPE for Friday at 00Z shows just upwards of 1000 \nJ/kg with 30 knots of 0-500mb shear. Clear skies ahead of this may \nincrease daytime heating and lead to better convection chances later \nin the day, but as of now, the CWA is categorized in SPC's Day 3 \nGeneral Thunderstorms group. \n\nFor the weekend, given the slow-moving Canadian low to the north, \nSW'ly mid-level flow veers to zonal. Subsequent shortwaves bring \nperiods of some low chances of precipitation and thunderstorms \nSaturday through Monday, and temperatures should be close to \nseasonable.",
